text:
alex: Quick check — I'm about to land a TF change for the inference sts that tries to avoid the thundering herd during scale-ups and also rotates a secret. TL;DR: add a small start delay per-pod, set maxSurge to 1, use partitioned rolling update + preStop drain. anyone see an obvious footgun?

maria: remind me the secret rotation flow? is this a swap-in that requires a full restart or just env reload?

alex: it's a swap of the KMS-backed TLS cert secret (name: inference-tls). pods read cert on startup only. so we need a rolling restart to pick it up. hence partitioned rollout.

sam: thinking out loud — two concerns: 1) burst CPU when pods start simultaneously; 2) endpoint flapping if we evict too many.

jen: agree with sam. what's the proposed replica count / delay?

alex: plan: replicas 5 -> maxUnavailable 1, maxSurge 1, startupProbe 10s initialDelay + successThreshold 1, and a new init container that sleeps a jittered delay based on ordinal. terraform snippet below.

alex: ```hcl
resource "kubernetes_stateful_set" "inference" {
  metadata { name = "inference" }
  spec {
    replicas = 5
    update_strategy {
      type = "RollingUpdate"
      rolling_update {
        partition = 0
      }
    }
    template {
      spec {
        affinity { /* same as before */ }
        container {
          name  = "inference"
          image = "redwood/inference:stable"
          startup_probe {
            exec { command = ["/bin/healthcheck"] }
            initial_delay_seconds = 10
            period_seconds = 5
            failure_threshold = 6
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

maria: for the jittered start can we avoid an init that sleeps? prefer in-process backoff so liveness/startup semantics are clearer. but init is simpler to guarantee ordering.

sam: init sleep is ok here if we set it small and jittered. do something like: sleep $(( (ORDINAL * 7) % 13 + rand 0..3 )) seconds. keep <30s.

alex: implementation plan: add init container using downward API to read ordinal, compute jitter, sleep; also add preStop hook to drain gracefully (SIGTERM wait 45s). then rotate secret via TF by changing secret data and bumping the sts annotation to trigger rolling update.

jen: what's the exact annotation you plan to bump? we should use a well-known one so ops scripts can watch it.

alex: will set pod template annotation `inference.redwood/secret-version = "v2026-03-18-rot1"` after secret update. ops can watch that.

maria: terraform plan taken? can you paste the plan output for the sts parts? want to sanity-check no unintended replacement.

alex: plan attached in PR, but pasted relevant diff: +init_container, +lifecycle.preStop, +pod_template.annotations updated. no replace expected, just patch.

deploy-bot: deploy-check: terraform plan OK for resource kubernetes_stateful_set.inference (update in-place).

sam: 👍 canary suggestion — apply to a single AZ first (we have zone labels); do a small rollout by setting partition = replicas - 1 to keep all but one on old revision, then reduce partition stepwise.

alex: good call. I'll run partitioned rollout: start with partition=4, wait 10m health checks, then partition=3... etc.

jen: make sure console alerting includes token-level latency dashboard — first starts might show elevated tail latency. we'll want to suppress pagers for non-critical regressions during the rollout.

maria: also add temporary rate-limit (nginx) backpressure if we see queue length spike.

alex: noted. I'll open a follow-up to add an nginx rate-limit config and the rollout playbook steps. aiming to apply the TF change in the next maintenance window (2am UTC).

sam: sounds good. ping me when you start and I'll watch.

jen: thanks, Alex. :thumbsup:

alex: thanks all — PR up: https://git.internal/redwood/infra/pull/4821 — will tag you once I start the partitioned rollout.
